WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:14.100
Africa is a continent of challenges, great
challenges.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:23.910
We’re talking about a continent that has
begun a process of modernization, but there’s

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:24.910
still a lot to do:

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:29.140
Develop a true health network; boost industry,
mechanize agriculture and, of course, respond

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:35.489
to the enormous challenge of improving education.

00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:38.930
If we look at results from recent decades
and identify the countries that have taken

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:43.070
the greatest economic leaps, we can see that
the common denominator has been an effort

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:47.379
to implement high-quality education systems.

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:52.300
In countries such as Japan, South Korea or
Singapore, the commitment to have first-rate

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.089
education systems really facilitated their
economic transition.

00:00:56.089 --> 00:01:00.410
Within a few years, all of these countries
went from poor, rudimentary economies to becoming

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:05.000
high value-added economic powers.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.850
That’s exactly what is happening now in
China.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:14.649
Of course, China’s success is due to many
factors, but throughout this entire process

00:01:14.649 --> 00:01:18.119
of economic takeoff, education played an important
role.

00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:23.350
And not only for economic reasons, but also
social, cultural and political ones.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:28.869
And now it's Africa’s turn.

00:01:28.869 --> 00:01:31.079
Listen up.

00:01:31.079 --> 00:01:35.069
(SCHOOLS THAT AREN’T SCHOOLS)

00:01:35.069 --> 00:01:39.929
Obviously, getting Sub-Saharan Africa to the
education level of developed countries will

00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:42.780
be a mammoth task that will take decades.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:47.679
And just as a house is constructed from the
foundations up, all kinds of public and private

00:01:47.679 --> 00:01:52.689
organizations have invested billions of dollars
in reducing illiteracy and improving schooling

00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:55.639
throughout sub-Saharan Africa for more than
three decades.

00:01:55.639 --> 00:02:01.889
However... the challenge is sooo enormous...
that there’s still a lot to do.

00:02:01.889 --> 00:02:06.210
For example, the lack of access to education
in Africa is one of its biggest problems.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:10.940
Despite all the efforts made, more than 20%
of children aged between 6 and 11 still don’t

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:15.870
go to school, and if we talk about children
aged between 12 and 14, the figure rises above

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.879
30%.

00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:23.400
The usual reason given for this situation
is the need in the poorest regions for children

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:30.790
to work in the fields from a young age and
the difficulty in meeting the costs of education.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:35.000
Because you see, even though the free public
education model has been adopted widely throughout

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.560
the African continent, parents still have
to pay expenses such as uniforms, shoes, books

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:40.560
or transportation.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:46.580
And, of course, in such a poor region this
is often quite complicated.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:50.490
But if you think that the lack of access or
not being able to pay for expenses are the

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:54.190
only problems hindering education in Africa,
you’re very, very wrong.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:58.310
The other great, and perhaps most significant,
challenge facing education systems in Africa

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:03.220
has to do with very poor teaching quality
and with the wasteful and uncontrolled use

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.220
of public funds.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:09.269
And many of you may be thinking, Simon, surely
you didn’t expect Africa to have Ivy-league

00:03:09.269 --> 00:03:11.760
class colleges after just a few years...

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.340
But wait, because when we talk about “poor
quality teachers” we mean the standard is

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:21.310
really low...

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:25.080
In many African countries, such as in Sierra
Leone, it’s common to find fake teachers.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:30.019
These are teachers who receive a salary from
the government but who neither teach nor work.

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:35.590
Since 2009 in Sierra Leone alone, more than
6,000 fake teachers have been detected and

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:38.180
eliminated from the state payroll.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.269
Although, to be fair, this doesn’t only
happen in Africa.

00:03:41.269 --> 00:03:44.090
It’s a frequent problem in other poor countries
too.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:48.530
In Pakistan, for example, there were more
than 8,000 schools that received funds from

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.939
the state but didn’t really exist.

00:03:50.939 --> 00:03:53.230
They were ghost schools.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:54.940
Where did that money go?

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:55.940
Better not to ask.

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:56.940
But that's not all.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:57.950
Even when teachers do show up...

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:00.670
Well let's just say that in Africa, not going
to school, skipping classes, often has more

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.930
to do with the teachers than the students.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:08.459
Check this out.

00:04:08.459 --> 00:04:17.780
Yes, in sub-Saharan Africa it’s common for
teachers to not show up regularly at school.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:22.490
Some studies show that in countries such as
Kenya or Nigeria this absenteeism affects

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:24.150
15% of the entire workforce.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:27.340
But if this already sounds crazy, listen up.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:32.690
In Kenya this percentage increases to almost
50% if we include teachers who go to school

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:35.490
but who don’t actually go into the classroom
to teach.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:40.900
And then there’s the fact that it isn’t
uncommon for many teachers who do teach to,

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:46.669
well… have difficulties reading or to not
really have a mastery of basic mathematics,

00:04:46.669 --> 00:04:48.340
even if they’re mathematics teachers.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:50.550
Basically... it’s a full-fledged disaster.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.930
And, no, opening more schools ISN’T enough.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:54.620
That won’t solve the problem.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:55.620
More things are needed.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:03.110
But wait just a second, because not all the
news is bad.

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:07.530
In addition to public schools and the schools
that NGOs have opened with cooperation aid,

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:12.919
there is another alternative that has recently
gained a strong following in the region:

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:13.919
Private low-cost education.

00:05:13.919 --> 00:05:14.919
Yes, yes, you heard that right.

00:05:14.919 --> 00:05:23.150
Private and low-cost education in the same
sentence.

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:26.509
Even though in many parts of the world private
education is reserved for people with high

00:05:26.509 --> 00:05:29.389
incomes, in Africa this barrier is eroding.

00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:34.009
Could private education help overcome the
problems of the continent's education systems?

00:05:34.009 --> 00:05:39.790
I’m sure you’ve never asked yourself that
question.

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:43.800
To delve deeper into this phenomenon and to
figure out what’s going on, in this video

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:49.490
we’ll focus on the examples of two very
different countries: Kenya and Liberia.

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:52.819
Kenya is one of the stars of Africa’s takeoff.

00:05:52.819 --> 00:05:56.810
Since 2013, its economy has been growing at
an average of more than 5% per year.

00:05:56.810 --> 00:06:01.050
Liberia, on the other hand, is still one of
the ten poorest countries in the world and

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:04.900
is bogged down with a lot of social and economic
problems.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:05.900
So..

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:09.860
What is a country as poor as Liberia doing
with private education?

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:15.830
How and why would a struggling country promote
a private education model?

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:19.580
Listen up.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:25.241
(LIBERIA, AWARE OF THE DISASTER)

00:06:25.241 --> 00:06:29.389
The Liberian government has noticed that it
has a huge problem, so huge that it’s practically

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:34.430
impossible for them to manage it themselves.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:40.211
After 14 years of civil war and constant epidemics,
education has remained a second-, third- or

00:06:40.211 --> 00:06:41.449
fourth-level priority.

00:06:41.449 --> 00:06:46.000
Add to this uncontrolled corruption and the
failure of public services is evident.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.759
Of course disastrous management goes hand-in-hand
with disastrous results.

00:06:49.759 --> 00:06:53.669
However, the Liberian government has decided
to break the mould and bet on a fairly novel

00:06:53.669 --> 00:07:03.569
way to ensure that the poorest have access
to better education.

00:07:03.569 --> 00:07:08.320
The people responsible for this strategy change
are the largest company dedicated to low-cost

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:14.030
private education in Africa: The Bridge International
Academies, a company founded in 2008 in Kenya

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:18.040
by two American friends.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:22.040
It currently has more than 600 schools that
offer education to more than 120,000 students.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:26.500
But, of course, it isn’t the only company
dedicated to this activity, not at all.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:31.290
There’s also the Rising Academies, another
of the main low-cost private school companies

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:34.360
in Africa, which was founded by British and
Canadian entrepreneurs.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:40.580
But… as we don’t have time to talk about
all the emerging companies, we’re going

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:43.690
to focus today on Bridge, which is the largest
of them all.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:46.030
And the first question we need to ask ourselves
is...

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:47.039
How is it funded?

00:07:47.039 --> 00:07:50.680
Well... nowadays it can continue operating
mostly thanks to the support of institutions

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:54.780
such as the World Bank and with donations
from business heavyweights such as Mark Zuckerberg

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:58.660
or Bill Gates, the founders of Facebook and
Microsoft.

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:01.810
Because, in most cases, these kinds of educational
companies aren’t profitable.

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:05.860
They only appear to be viable because of the
donations they receive.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:09.050
For example, let’s take the case of Bridge
International.

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:13.460
In 2016 they declared an income of 16 million
dollars but had operating losses of 12 million.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:18.879
That means their total costs were a whopping
28 million, meaning their costs were 75% greater

00:08:18.879 --> 00:08:19.889
than their income.

00:08:19.889 --> 00:08:24.820
But, just a second, don’t think we’re
talking about something like an NGO, not at

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:25.820
all.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:26.820
These companies are for-profit.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.680
What happens is that they conceive a long-term
business model.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:33.240
They hope that the economic development that
has begun in many African regions will soon

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:37.039
allow them to reap the benefits.

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:41.099
And of course, in the meantime they receive
support in the form of multiple donations,

00:08:41.099 --> 00:08:44.820
such as those of Bill Gates, that more than
compensate for all business losses.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:48.920
But they often receive these donations precisely
because they have a feasibility plan.

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:53.790
That is, because they expect it to be a profitable,
and therefore autonomous and sustainable project

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:56.850
in the medium-term.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:00.990
Of course, spending is easy but managing to
create a self-sustaining project… that's

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:01.990
something else.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:06.130
And it is precisely for this reason, and despite
all these losses, that the company continues

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:07.320
with its expansion plan.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:13.339
It’s all thanks to the more than 140 million
dollars it has earned in investments and donations.

00:09:13.339 --> 00:09:14.760
Surprised?

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:20.840
The fact is that in 2016, the government of
Liberia, a country where less than 40% of

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:24.440
children receive primary education, decided
to work with Bridge International.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:25.440
Why?

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:30.980
Well... because obvious that public education
wasn’t working in this country.

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:35.529
(“Teachers don’t show up, even though
they’re paid by the government.

00:09:35.529 --> 00:09:36.790
There are no books.

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:39.209
Training is very weak.

00:09:39.209 --> 00:09:40.800
School infrastructure is not safe.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:42.600
We have to do something radical.”

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:49.500
- George Werner, ex-minister of Education
of Liberia.)

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:52.850
The point is that the Liberian government
signed an agreement for Bridge International

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:56.920
to operate 93 public schools with more than
27,000 students.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:00.300
Access to these schools is still free, the
government pays for the students.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:04.011
To date, this is the largest agreement signed
between an African government and a private

00:10:04.011 --> 00:10:05.140
education company.

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:09.250
And this experiment seems to be quite a success.

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:13.240
In level tests, students from schools managed
by Bridge International achieve much, much

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:16.430
better results than students from schools
operated by the state.

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:20.060
To give you an idea of the divide, in the
level tests the Bridge students’ results

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:21.920
can be 100% higher.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:26.600
That is, Bridge students do twice as well
as students from government-controlled schools.

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:31.060
But... just a second, because in spite of
these positive results, not every country

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:34.010
is making things easy for this type of education
business.

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:39.420
Take for example Kenya, the very country where
Bridge International was born.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:43.160
Listen up.

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:46.910
(KENYA'S MISTRUST)

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:47.910
Let's not fool ourselves.

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:52.370
Private schools can pose a threat to state-controlled
education and many governments don’t like

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:53.370
that.

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:54.680
Neither do public school teachers.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:59.760
That’s exactly what’s happening in Kenya
where the National Teachers' Union has been

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:04.570
conducting a very tough campaign against the
low-cost private schools’ model for years.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:07.950
Supposedly they are against the teachers in
these types of centers who don’t have a

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:09.850
government license.

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:13.180
Even though the students’ academic results
are much better.

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:16.230
(“Bridge is unauthorised and illegal".

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:20.839
- Wilson Sossion, secretary-general of the
Kenya National Union of Teachers.)

00:11:20.839 --> 00:11:22.399
There is some truth however to their arguments.

00:11:22.399 --> 00:11:25.010
The company itself recognizes it.

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:29.340
(‘Technically, we’re breaking the law
but so are thousands of other schools who

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:30.960
are operating like this”.

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:34.320
- Shannon May, co-founder of Bridge International
Academies.)

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:37.980
Among other things, the educational programs
aren’t written in the country they’re

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:42.260
destined for, nor do they meet the local governments’
requirements, they’re usually written abroad.

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:46.060
In Bridge’s case, educational performance
is even evaluated by Harvard professors.

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:50.350
Which, to tell the truth, and without meaning
to offend anyone, is probably not such a bad

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:54.029
thing when you consider Kenya’s politicians.

00:11:54.029 --> 00:11:58.660
Of course, not everything is rosy with the
private model.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:01.260
And sometimes unpleasant situations occur.

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:05.079
Companies like Bridge International are still
for-profit companies, which requires parents

00:12:05.079 --> 00:12:06.190
to keep up with payments.

00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:09.389
And that can be a struggle in a place like
Africa.

00:12:09.389 --> 00:12:12.410
However, what’s the alternative?

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:16.010
Public schools where teachers go to class
when they feel like it or where math classes

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:18.180
are taught by people who hardly know how to
multiply?

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:22.630
In sub-Saharan Africa, governments have proven
very ineffective in providing public services.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:24.930
That's why many alternatives are emerging.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:28.930
In some cases, they are companies and in others
they are non-profit organizations.

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:33.670
The important thing is that these initiatives
are revolutionizing education in Africa.

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:35.630
(Low-cost schools are transforming Africa.

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:36.630
- Forbes)

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:40.430
Could it be that investing in private low-cost
schools that aim to be profitable is one of

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:42.850
the best ways to help develop quality education
in Africa?

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:46.089
Could it be that investments are better than
mere handouts?

00:12:46.089 --> 00:12:47.500
Leave your answer in the comments.

